// TASK 4: Leaked Credentials and Breach Data
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>When Someone Else's Breach Becomes Your Problem</h2>

    <p>A <strong>leaked credential</strong> is different from a default one. It is a real username and password that a person actually chose, which was later exposed when a service they used was breached. The password itself was never weak by design, it was simply stolen.</p>

    <p>The danger shows up through <strong>password reuse</strong>. If someone uses the same password for their email, their bank, and their work account, then a breach at any one of those three exposes all three. Attackers automate this by taking a breached list of usernames and passwords and trying them against many other sites at once, a technique called <strong>credential stuffing</strong>.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Breach Lookup Services</h4>
        <p>A breach lookup service lets someone check whether their email address or username has appeared in a known data breach. These services typically confirm exposure and the type of data involved, they do not hand back the actual leaked password.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Two Different Origins</h3>
    <!-- SVG: default vs leaked credential comparison
