// TASK 6: Conclusion
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <p>Default and leaked credentials are two of the simplest ways an attacker gains initial access, and both are entirely preventable. A default credential survives only because nobody changed it. A leaked credential spreads only because a password was reused somewhere else.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Default credentials are factory set logins shared across every unit of a device model.</li>
        <li>Default credential databases let testers check known factory logins before attempting anything else.</li>
        <li>Leaked credentials come from real users and are exposed through data breaches.</li>
        <li>Credential stuffing automates trying leaked logins across many other services.</li>
        <li>Changing default passwords and avoiding password reuse closes both of these attack paths.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
HTML;

